; Follow with the off. Winning 42 p. 24 (S-0040-a); played in Hand 30 trick
; 2 (S-0135-g), and in Hand 16 trick 2.
; Rule, in our words: the bidder, following a suit he cannot win, plays an
; off of that suit whose own suit's double he lacks: the first time the suit
; is led is the time to lose it, while the others still hold tiles of it. An
; off his double protects he keeps. In Hand 30 he follows his partner's 0-0
; with the 1-0 and keeps the 2-0, which the 2-2 protects; in Hand 16 he
; follows the setter's 1-1 with the 1-0 and keeps the 2-1 under his 2-2.
; Reading: S-0040-a's sentence reads two ways: as the bidder's lead, the off
; before a double of another suit (bidders-plan.silas, S-0040-b), and as his
; follow, which this file encodes. The follow reading never plays the off
; where the bidder can take the trick: with the double of the suit led he
; plays the double (p. 11, S-0024-a), which helper-stretch.silas reads where
; no setter can trump.
; The off is judged in the suit followed as well as its own: the tile played
; must be one that can lose there (Hand 30's 1-0 on the 0-0, Hand 16's 1-0 on
; the 1-1). A tile no live tile beats in the suit led is no off on that trick
; (2026-10-14, #146; before, only its own suit was asked). The book does not
; say whether an off that is itself count goes on a trick the setters are
; taking (GAPS.md, the book's silences); the rule still plays it.
; New: no gate has it.

(include book)

(policy follow-with-the-off
  (rule play-the-bare-off (play o)
    (viewer b)
    (bidder b)
    (led-context q)
    (not (trump q))
    (not (can-take-it))
    (follower o)
    (loses-in o q)
    (bare-off o)))
